  "body": "# C06: Write-guard primitive for generation, cancellation and lease windows\n\nPart of #902; implements #956. Depends on C05c (#968).\n\nProvide the synchronous guard and control errors required by `docs/coordinators.md:187–194`. C07/C08 install publication hooks and propagate the errors. C06 remains disabled in production under `docs/coordinators.md:338–352`.\n\nCurrent main supplies the required seams:\n\n- `ClaimClient` has classified read/write outcomes, including `applied | unchanged | moved | unconfirmed`; ambiguous writes are re-read (`src/integrations/claim-ref.ts:12–30`, `src/integrations/claim-ref.ts:222–229`).\n- `LeaseHandle` binds repository, ref, run, member and generation. `LeaseManager.windowOpen` checks current confirmed ownership, matching anchor and current clocks (`src/integrations/leases.ts:14–36`, `src/integrations/leases.ts:76–85`).\n- The clock window requires an anchor, inclusive elapsed time below 420,000 ms and suspend divergence at most 1,000 ms (`src/util/claim-clock.ts:64–83`).\n- Local rows separate confirmed ownership from pending intent (`src/integrations/lease-store.ts:11–20`). Manager construction performs recovery before returning; `start` delivers buffered losses (`src/integrations/leases.ts:63–68`, `src/integrations/leases.ts:236–247`).\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Synchronous and read-only.** `claimGuard(): void` performs no await, network call, renewal or SQLite mutation. Returning permits only the immediately following operation; it creates no reusable authorization.\n\n2. **Every claim must permit the write.** Each binding retains its original identity. Every guard call reads live cancellation and checks `manager.windowOpen(capturedHandle)`. An identity, pending acquisition or persisted `held` row alone grants no authority.\n\n3. **Deterministic error precedence.**\n   - First inspect every loss latch. Any known loss throws `ClaimLostError`.\n   - Otherwise evaluate cancellation probes across the scope. Propagate a probe-thrown `ClaimControlError` unchanged. Otherwise any literal `true` yields `\"cancelled\"`; then any exception or nonboolean yields `\"unavailable\"`.\n   - Only if all cancellations are literal `false`, evaluate window probes. Propagate a probe-thrown control error unchanged. Otherwise any exception or nonboolean yields `\"unavailable\"`; then any literal `false` yields `\"lease\"`.\n   - Only all literal `true` windows permit proceeding. Neither error changes run status or publishes anything.\n\n4. **Loss belongs to one identity.** Match canonical repository, ref, run, member and generation. Matching notifications permanently latch loss and are idempotent. A successor cannot revive an old binding; an old notification cannot poison its successor.\n\n5. **Async scope isolation.** Nested scopes add claims to their parent; an empty child cannot remove parent requirements. Descendants retain inherited bindings across awaits and delayed callbacks. Returning or throwing restores the caller’s scope.\n\n6. **Maintenance remains possible.** Claim IO and lease maintenance run through native AsyncLocalStorage `exit`. This clears only the claim scope, preserves other async-local contexts and restores the caller afterward.\n\n7. **Absent scope is inert.** With no bindings, return without reading cancellation, clocks or lease state. Shared mode stays off by default.\n\n## Mechanism\n\n### Control errors\n\nAdd `src/integrations/claim-errors.ts` with no imports, including type imports:\n\n```ts\nexport type ClaimWaitReason = \"cancelled\" | \"lease\" | \"unavailable\";\n\nexport abstract class ClaimControlError extends Error {\n  protected constructor(message: string);\n}\n\nexport class ClaimWaitError extends ClaimControlError {\n  readonly reason: ClaimWaitReason;\n  constructor(reason: ClaimWaitReason);\n}\n\nexport class ClaimLostError extends ClaimControlError {\n  constructor();\n}\n\nexport function rethrowClaimControl(error: unknown): void;\n```\n\nConcrete errors set `name` to their class name and use fixed messages. Accept no caller-supplied diagnostic text, identity, response or cause.\n\n`rethrowClaimControl` throws the same object for `instanceof ClaimControlError`; otherwise it returns. Do not inspect names, messages or nested causes. C07/C08 add this call before existing catch handling.\n\n### Bindings, scope and guard\n\nAdd `src/integrations/claim-guard.ts`. Runtime imports are limited to `node:async_hooks` and `claim-errors.ts`; lease and claim imports are type-only.\n\n```ts\nexport interface ScopedClaim {\n  readonly handle: LeaseHandle;\n  readonly lost: boolean;\n  cancelled(): boolean;\n  windowOpen(): boolean;\n  onLost(h: LeaseHandle, tip: ClaimTip | null): void;\n}\n\nexport function bindClaim(\n  leases: Pick\u003cLeaseManager, \"windowOpen\"\u003e,\n  handle: LeaseHandle,\n  cancelled: () =\u003e boolean,\n): ScopedClaim;\n\nexport const claimScope: {\n  run\u003cT\u003e(claims: readonly ScopedClaim[], fn: () =\u003e T): T;\n  exit\u003cT\u003e(fn: () =\u003e T): T;\n};\n\nexport function claimGuard(): void;\n```\n\n`bindClaim` accepts an expected identity obtained from a manager result or reconstructed by daemon recovery code from trusted local lease/run records and the member identity supplied to the manager. Copy and freeze the identity; return a frozen binding with live probes and a closure-backed loss latch. Never substitute a newer handle.\n\nRecovery wiring must capture expected identities **before constructing the manager**, because construction can change those records. After construction returns, create and register the bindings before calling `start`. This requires no handle enumeration API or registry and adds no production wiring in C06.\n\n`onLost` compares all five identity fields, comparing repository case-insensitively. Ignore and do not retain the tip, including `null`. Matching delivery latches loss without throwing into maintenance.\n\nUse a private `AsyncLocalStorage\u003creadonly ScopedClaim[]\u003e`. `run` snapshots and freezes the combined parent-plus-child array and returns the callback result unchanged. Binding state remains live.\n\n`exit` delegates to native `scope.exit(fn)`. Starting an async operation as `scope.exit(() =\u003e operation())` is valid: its continuations inherit the cleared scope after awaits. Passing an already-started promise through `exit` does not clear that operation’s context.\n\n`claimGuard` implements invariant 3. Collect ordinary results across each probe stage before deciding, so array order cannot change reason precedence; immediately rethrow probe-thrown control errors. Discard other exception text.\n\nC07/C08 call the guard after preparatory awaits and immediately before send/spawn. The primitive cannot retract in-flight requests or provide GitHub-enforced generation fencing for comments and PR creation (`docs/coordinators.md:187–191`).\n\n### Maintenance exemptions\n\nPreserve existing signatures, classifications and lease policy:\n\n- Run each complete returned `ClaimClient.read` and `write` operation through `exit`, including continuations and ambiguous-result reads.\n- Exempt manager `acquire`, `update`, `release` and `start`, plus the factory’s `recoverLeases` call.\n- Explicitly exempt each renewal tick, even when invoked from a scoped callback; do not rely only on timer-registration context.\n- Leave `windowOpen` scoped: it is the guard’s synchronous inspection.\n- Keep the existing canonical-key mutex and reconciliation inside it (`src/integrations/leases.ts:51–74`, `src/integrations/leases.ts:104–110`). Release must still revoke its window synchronously before queued work; an older renewal response must not restore it (`src/integrations/leases.ts:187–192`, `src/integrations/leases.ts:216–223`).\n\nThese exemptions allow maintenance to execute; they preserve CAS, time checks and durable intents.\n\nC06 does not reinterpret lease outcomes. `unchanged` and `unconfirmed` do not refresh anchors or imply loss; uncertainty retains its intent and existing confirmed authority. An `applied` result with a null anchor cannot open a window. Same-holder `moved` is not loss; confirmed supersession is reported through `onLost`.\n\nBindings, latches and anchors are process-local. Recovered ownership remains blocked without a fresh usable window. The guard checks current injected clocks through the manager; it does not decide ten-minute expiry or add recovery, replay or retry policy.\n\n## Tests\n\nUse `test/claim-guard.test.ts` for the primitive. Extend existing `test/leases.test.ts` fixtures for manager behavior, especially tests 10–12 below; add guard assertions rather than duplicate their race, outcome or restart matrices.\n\nUse fake GitHub, injected clients, clocks and timers, local stores and deferred promises. No network, live models or clock sleeps. Demonstrate that each named mutation fails its corresponding case.\n\n1. **Authority.** With cancellation false and clocks inside the window, a matching held binding permits a fake publication. Wrong run/member/generation, absent or never-confirmed ownership, and released/lost ownership without notification each throw `\"lease\"` before publication. **Mutation:** skip the manager check or replace it with a clock-only check.\n\n2. **Live cancellation.** Change cancellation after a deferred await inside a valid scope. The next guard throws `\"cancelled\"` and sends nothing. **Mutation:** capture cancellation at binding or scope creation.\n\n3. **All claims and nesting.** Two valid claims permit publication; closing only the second blocks it. A closed parent blocks an empty or valid child. **Mutation:** inspect only one claim or replace inherited bindings.\n\n4. **Async isolation.** Interleave open and closed scopes across awaits; delayed descendants retain their original bindings. Verify restoration after return, throw and rejected promise. Outside scopes, probes that would throw are never called. **Mutation:** use global current bindings, lose restoration or reject absent scope.\n\n5. **Loss identity.** Matching notification, including `tip: null`, latches loss; duplicates are harmless. Vary each identity field independently to prove mismatches do not latch; repository case variants match. Loss in a later binding beats earlier cancellation, unavailable probes or closed windows. A successor remains usable. **Mutation:** omit an identity field, clear the latch or probe before checking all losses.\n\n6. **Control-error identity.** Concrete errors and a test subclass of `ClaimControlError` rethrow by identity. Ordinary errors, matching-name objects and wrapped causes return normally. **Mutation:** wrap, swallow or classify by text/name.\n\n7. **Clock and probe precedence.** A real manager permits at 419,999 ms and blocks at 420,000 ms; below that age, 1,001 ms suspend divergence blocks. Throwing clocks/predicates and nonboolean probe results fail closed without leaking text. In both binding orders, cancellation `true` beats an ordinary cancellation failure; cancellation failure prevents window evaluation; window failure beats window `false`. Probe-thrown control errors retain identity even alongside ordinary results. **Mutation:** cache permission, short-circuit on ordinary results or change precedence.\n\n8. **Independently observable maintenance exemptions.** Test client exemptions with a guard-probing `GhRunner`, including probes after deferred awaits and during ambiguous-result reads. Separately test manager operations and factory recovery with an injected `ClaimClient` that calls the guard before and after an await, without client exemptions. Probe `start` in injected timer registration; invoke its captured tick from a blocked scope. Assert exact expected outcomes and per-fixture request counts: successful acquisition is `held`, update is `\"applied\"`, release is `\"released\"`, and the active tick performs its expected renewal. A caught guard error classified as `\"unchanged\"`, `\"unconfirmed\"` or `\"wait\"` is failure. Preserve an unrelated async-local sentinel throughout; the caller remains blocked afterward. **Mutation:** remove each exemption individually, start work before `exit`, exempt only its first request or clear scope globally.\n\n9. **Renewal versus expiry.** From a closed scope, a usable renewable GitHub-time sample lets `update` return `\"applied\"` and the next guard pass. An unusable sample returns `\"wait\"`, sends no claim CAS, emits no loss and leaves the guard blocked. A usable sample proving expiry returns `\"lost\"` without renewal; its matching notification makes the guard throw `ClaimLostError`. A fresh generation cannot revive that binding. **Mutation:** block maintenance, manufacture renewal authority or equate unusable time with expiry.\n\n10. **Immediate release and stale callbacks.** Extend the queued-renewal/release fixture with guard assertions immediately after requesting release and after the older renewal completes. Both remain blocked. Extend the obsolete-handle fixture with a delayed generation-1 callback after generation 2 opens: the old binding cannot publish. **Mutation:** cache permission, replace the captured handle or restore a revoked anchor.\n\n11. **Outcome boundary.** Add guard assertions to existing uncertain-outcome, equivalent-record and moved-tip cases: neither `unchanged` nor `unconfirmed` refreshes the window; uncertainty retains its intent; null-anchor confirmation stays blocked; same-holder contention does not latch loss, while delivered supersession does. **Mutation:** treat an attempt/readback as an anchor or every non-success as loss.\n\n12. **Recovery bindings.** Extend existing recovery fixtures: capture trusted identities before manager construction, bind after it returns, and route buffered losses before starting scoped work. Recovered held ownership without an anchor, unresolved intent and recovered release remain blocked; matching recovered loss throws `ClaimLostError`. A fresh usable renewal opens only its current generation. **Mutation:** derive identities only after recovery, miss buffered loss or restore authority from a persisted row alone.\n\n## Compatibility\n\n- Shared mode remains off. No production scope, publication hook or new run precondition is installed.\n- No migration, persisted field, config key, HTTP shape, claim format or manifest change. Previous releases continue using the same database after rollback.\n- Older local runs retain their existing resume path.\n- Enabled shared-work rollback remains C24’s drain/cutover concern (`docs/coordinators.md:311–320`).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **C07/C08:** publication hooks, catch propagation, run-status mapping, exact-SHA enforcement and production caller inventory.\n- **C11/C13:** checkpoint/acquisition wiring, adoption and return quarantine; no recovery handle registry or manager API expansion here.\n- **C12:** durable publication effects and crash reconciliation. C06 does not reproduce publication crash matrices.\n- **C14/C15/C17/C19:** capability admission, drain policy, land-lock wiring and shared cancellation transport.\n- **C24:** enabling shared admission and rollback/cutover validation.\n- New lease policy, persistence, retries or replay behavior.\n\n## Budget\n\nAbout **150 changed source lines plus tests**, counting edits to existing modules: fixed control errors, bindings/scope/guard and narrow maintenance exemptions.\n\n**Hard ceiling: 202 changed source lines. Stop and report before exceeding it.** This stays below the design’s 250-line per-slice cap (`docs/coordinators.md:338–340`). Keep deferred wiring in its named slices.\n\n\n_Spec-reviewed 2026-10-29: no SPEC-P1; every SPEC-P2 resolved in this text._\n"
